Coeliac Disease (Classical GI Phenotype)
Complex Fatigue Entities
Gluten-induced autoimmune enteropathy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-009
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe gastrointestinal distress, intractable diarrhea, and hypovolemia risk following accidental gluten ingestion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated explosive diarrhea, severe abdominal cramping, and presyncope occurring hours after a restaurant meal. Immediate physical evaluation was mandated to assess hydration status, rule out acute surgical abdomen, and administer parenteral symptom control prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I ate something cross-contaminated, and I've been on the toilet with severe cramps and diarrhea for 6 hours; I feel like I'm going to pass out.
History of Present Illness: Patient with known Coeliac disease presents with a severe acute flare triggered by accidental gluten exposure earlier today. Symptoms include classic gluten-induced autoimmune enteropathy manifestations: massive watery diarrhea, severe periumbilical abdominal cramping, and intense nausea. The patient reports profound lethargy and progressive dizziness upon standing.
Functional Status: Confined to bed/bathroom due to frequent bowel motions and presyncope.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, unable to maintain oral fluid intake due to nausea, rapidly dehydrating.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 mmHg | HR: 108 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Pale, diaphoretic, dry mucous membranes, clutching abdomen in distress.
Physical Exam: Tachycardia with delayed capillary refill (3 seconds). Abdomen is diffusely tender with severe hyperactive bowel sounds, but soft, non-distended, with no guarding or rebound tenderness. Skin turgor is sluggish. Severe malabsorption and villous atrophy cascade clinically evident.
Environmental Observations: Easy access to bathroom. Water bottles present but untouched.
Medication Reconciliation: No current active medications. Strict gluten-free diet normally maintained.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Coeliac Disease (Classical GI Phenotype) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Diffuse pain rather than localized right lower quadrant pain, absence of rebound tenderness, and classical prior history of identical gluten-exposure flares.

Ischemic bowel - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is proportional to findings, lack of significant cardiovascular risk factors, and clear dietary trigger.

Infectious gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Rapid onset directly following potential cross-contamination, without fever or sick contacts.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with rapid-onset, massive fluid loss via severe diarrhea, resulting in clinical hypovolemia and presyncope. Urgent after-hours assessment was vital to clinically exclude an acute surgical abdomen and objectively measure hemodynamic stability. Delaying care would have predictably led to severe dehydration and circulatory collapse requiring emergency ambulance transfer. Immediate administration of parenteral antiemetics and gut-motility modulators was essential to halt fluid loss in the home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ondansetron 4mg sublingually and Loperamide 4mg orally to halt diarrhea and nausea. Prescribed oral rehydration solution. Targeted intervention strategy includes strict GF diet, Zinc, Magnesium, and Vitamin D.
Positive Behavioral Change: By slowly sipping 50ml of oral rehydration fluid every 10 minutes, you will maximize mucosal absorption without triggering vomiting, directly replenishing your blood volume and rewarding yourself with immediate relief from dizziness.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None acutely.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review in 3-5 days to monitor nutritional status and arrange follow-up micronutrient blood panels.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to ED if inability to tolerate any fluids persists for 12 hours, if blood appears in the stool, or if actual syncope occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute flares are highly distressing but functionally self-limiting once the antigenic trigger clears the gastrointestinal tract.
Clinical Course: Diarrhea and cramping should substantially resolve within 24-48 hours with medication. Fatigue and brain fog may linger for several days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 or go to the ED if you pass out completely or if you start vomiting blood.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The medications directly paralyze the hyperactive gut muscle and turn off the brain's nausea center, allowing you to drink the fluids required to fix your low blood pressure.
Actionable Steps: Restrict diet to clear, electrolyte-rich fluids for the next 24 hours; completely resting the inflamed gut lining allows it to begin healing, positively reinforcing your rapid recovery.

